# run_osot_optimize.ps1
# Delivered to C:\Temp by roles/osot_optimize/tasks/main.yml's own
# "Deliver OSOT Optimize script to C:\Temp" task. This file exists so an
# interactive console test is a TRUE apples-to-apples comparison against
# the automated run, not a manually-retyped approximation that could
# quietly drift out of sync - "Start OSOT Optimize pass" in tasks/main.yml
# runs this exact same file over WinRM; to test it interactively instead,
# log on to the VM's console (or RDP) and run, from an elevated PowerShell
# prompt:
#
#   powershell.exe -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File C:\Temp\run_osot_optimize.ps1
#
# Do NOT manually reboot the VM while an Ansible-launched pass is still
# in progress/pending, even to check results faster - that breaks the
# WinRM connection tasks/main.yml's own "Wait for OSOT Optimize log to
# report completion" task is still polling through, and kills the whole
# build/VM, same as killing the process outright does.
#
# HISTORY (condensed - see tasks/main.yml's own header for the full,
# still-current account of what was tried and why):
#   - Originally a single synchronous win_shell -> hung. Root cause:
#     Ansible's WinRM connection plugin cutting off a long command
#     regardless of task-level timeouts (ansible_winrm_operation_timeout_sec
#     defaults to 20s, read to 30s - ansible/ansible issue #81095). Fixed,
#     at the time, by tasks/main.yml running this script async + polling
#     via async_status - since replaced (see "CURRENT FIX" below) by
#     controller-side polling against OSOT's own log file instead, which
#     sidesteps the same class of problem without needing async at all.
#   - -r (report file) REMOVED - tested for real against a genuinely
#     successful Optimize pass (OSOT GUI reopened afterward showed 495/493
#     applied) with both a full file path and, per Omnissa's own CLI doc's
#     own contradictory example, a bare directory - neither ever produced a
#     file. OSOT already writes its own timestamped logs to
#     C:\ProgramData\Omnissa\Omnissa OS Optimization Tool\Log regardless of
#     any flag; tasks/main.yml's own "Copy OSOT's own Optimize log into
#     build_log_dir" task harvests that instead.
#   - -ArgumentList as an ARRAY (@(...)), not a manually-quoted single
#     string - each array element is its own separate argument, no manual
#     quote-escaping needed or wanted (MicrosoftDocs/PowerShell-Docs issue
#     #7701) - confirmed the hard way after a hand-quoted version broke at
#     a real VM console with "Unexpected token" on the escaped quotes.
#
# INVOCATION REWRITTEN 2026-09-15 (again) - was
# Start-Process -NoNewWindow -PassThru @('-o'), tried with a plain WinRM
# connection, then with become:true/runas/SYSTEM, then launched via a
# Scheduled Task instead of this script's own invocation changing at all -
# none of it got OSOT past its own "Omnissa General Terms" EULA dialog
# (SYSTEM in Session 0 has no window station to draw it on at all; a
# LogonType=Interactive Scheduled Task targeting the console's own
# Administrator session never actually left State=Queued, confirmed twice
# on a real build, once triggered locally at the console itself). Full
# account of all three attempts is in tasks/main.yml's own header - kept
# there rather than duplicated here since that's where the become/
# Scheduled Task/PsExec code itself used to live.
#
# The user then pointed at the Omnissa Community's own "Horizon Gold Image
# creation with Ansible" guide (community.omnissa.com, topic 72927), whose
# own working OSOT Optimize invocation is:
#   Start-Process "C:\Temp\<osot.exe>" -ArgumentList $arglist -Verb RunAs -Wait
# -Verb RunAs (ShellExecute-based elevation) instead of -NoNewWindow is the
# one, real, load-bearing difference worth adopting - they're mutually
# exclusive in Start-Process (can't combine -Verb RunAs with -NoNewWindow
# at all), so this is a genuine fork, not a tweak. -NoNewWindow's original
# rationale (Start-Process's default new-window behavior needing a window
# station a WinRM session doesn't have) didn't hold up against what was
# actually observed in Session 0/the Scheduled Task attempts - adopting
# -Verb RunAs is going on this guide's own proven, real-world result
# rather than further theorizing about why.
#
# NOT adopted from that guide: -v (verbose) - Omnissa's own KB 94531
# ("Omnissa Optimization Tool for Horizon: Compatibility Issues with
# Packer Automation") documents "-o ... -v" hanging at "Loading Templates"
# specifically when automated via Packer ("will function interactively,
# however will not function via Packer") - that guide's own pipeline isn't
# Packer-based (plain ansible-playbook against a running VM), this
# project's is, so that specific documented risk applies here and not
# there. Also NOT adopted: the guide's own `exit $LASTEXITCODE` check after
# Start-Process - $LASTEXITCODE is NOT set by Start-Process at all (only by
# direct/call-operator native invocation - Start-Process's own -PassThru
# process object's ExitCode property is the only reliable read), so this
# keeps the already-correct -PassThru/.ExitCode pattern below rather than
# copying a check that likely never actually fires in the guide's own
# pipeline either. Also NOT adopted (yet): -t/explicit template - still
# omitted, relying on the documented default-template fallback; see
# tasks/main.yml's own header for a corrected template-string candidate
# from this same guide, worth trying separately rather than changing two
# things in the same test.
#
# rc 3010 (ERROR_SUCCESS_REBOOT_REQUIRED) is treated as success alongside 0
# by tasks/main.yml's own failed_when now, adopted from the same guide - a
# legitimate Windows convention this project's check was missing before.
#
# -Wait REMOVED 2026-09-15 - -Verb RunAs got OSOT itself all the way to a
# genuinely completed run for the first time (confirmed on a real build:
# Task Scheduler's own Omnissa\OptimizationToolTask entries ran and were
# cleaned up same as the original successful test; Optimize_*.log fully
# populated with hundreds of real actions, ending in an "htmlReport:" line;
# a reopened OSOT GUI Analyze showed 496/494 applied) - but
# Start-Process ... -Wait never returned even so, leaving the pipeline
# stuck at "Wait for OSOT Optimize pass to finish" indefinitely despite the
# real work being done. Two plausible, not mutually exclusive causes: OSOT
# itself may simply not self-terminate after -o finishes (the ORIGINAL
# successful interactive test earlier this session also needed the user to
# manually reopen OSOT afterward to check Analyze results - consistent with
# the process/its GUI staying resident rather than exiting on its own), or
# -Verb RunAs's own -Wait is unreliable across UAC's elevation handoff (a
# general, documented PowerShell quirk, independent of this specific tool).
# Either way, trusting Start-Process's own exit-tracking isn't safe here.
#
# FIRST FIX (superseded, see below): launch without -Wait, then have this
# script itself poll its own Optimize_*.log in a while loop for the
# "htmlReport:" completion line, stopping the process once found. Tested
# for real on the next build: confirmed, via the user's own direct check,
# that the Optimize_*.log genuinely DID reach htmlReport: and osot.exe was
# genuinely gone from Task Manager - the work was done, cleanly - but
# tasks/main.yml's own "Wait for OSOT Optimize pass to finish" (at the
# time, an async_status poll) STILL never saw it as finished. So the
# detection target itself was never wrong - it's that trusting ONE
# long-lived async-wrapped background PowerShell script (this script's own
# inner poll loop, sitting in Start-Sleep for however long OSOT takes) to
# reliably report itself finished back to Ansible's async_status isn't
# working, for reasons not worth chasing further when a more robust
# pattern already exists elsewhere in this same pipeline.
#
# CURRENT FIX 2026-09-15: this script goes back to just launching OSOT and
# returning immediately - no internal loop, no Stop-Process, no throw.
# Completion detection AND process cleanup both moved to
# tasks/main.yml's own "Wait for OSOT Optimize log to report completion"
# and "Ensure the OSOT process is no longer running" tasks instead, which
# poll/act from the Ansible controller side via many short, fresh,
# independent WinRM calls - the same idiom this pipeline's own file-gate
# pauses and the original async_status loop both already used
# successfully, rather than one script that has to stay alive and
# self-report for however long OSOT takes. See that file's own header for
# the full reasoning. This script's only remaining job is the launch
# itself, so a WinRM call running it returns in well under a second.
$Optimizer = "C:\Temp\{{ osot_installer | basename }}"
Start-Process -FilePath $Optimizer -ArgumentList @('-o') -Verb RunAs
